From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@invalid>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 20:13:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] clutter/color: Map highlights toward a declared target
 luminance
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add the Report ITU-R BT.2390 EETF as a color op, and apply it in a
color transform whose target state declares a target luminance (the
blending state of an output whose target peak is known) when the
source's effective peak exceeds it.

The op, bt2390_eetf, works on max (R, G, B) of linear BT.2020 and
scales all three channels by the same ratio, so hue and saturation
ratios are kept, negative components stay negative and a sample with
no positive component is left unchanged. On that luminance it applies
the BT.2390 curve in the PQ domain, compressing highlights only: the
source range [Lsrc_min, Lsrc_peak] and the target peak converted to PQ,
normalization by the source range, a Hermite spline above
KS = 1.5 maxLum - 0.5, and denormalization. There is no black level
lift (b = 0) and the target minimum is not used: a lift would raise
black, and, applied as a ratio on max (R, G, B), would scale near-black
samples, negative components included, by large factors. Below the
knee the curve is the identity, down to and below the source minimum,
without a PQ round trip or a division; above the source peak it stays
at the target peak. A knee at or above 1 (the source peak within a
rounding error of the target) leaves the curve the identity. A sample
whose max (R, G, B) is not positive is left unchanged before any
division. The op has a CPU and a GLSL implementation with the same
maths and is internal, like the HLG ops.

It is placed in the surface to blending-state transform after the
existing reference-white scaling and before the conversion out of
linear BT.2020. Whether it is needed is one exported predicate,
clutter_color_transform_requires_highlight_mapping(), which the
transform evaluates before its shortcut for equal encodings, both to
decide that a pipeline is needed although the encodings are equal and
to add the op, and which libmutter will use to refuse direct scanout.
The source peak is the single definition,
clutter_color_state_params_get_peak_headroom(): a source whose peak is
unknown (extended-range linear content declaring neither MaxCLL nor a
mastering maximum) is not mapped.

The color transform cache is now keyed by exact identity:
clutter_color_state_identical(), exported, and a matching hash, defined
once, compare the resolved luminances bitwise, the target volume and the
display reference. clutter_color_state_equals() treats luminances within
10% as equal, so two states whose transforms differ, in whether or how
they map highlights notably, could otherwise share a cached transform.
The public equality is unchanged.

Luminance units. Every parametric state normalizes its linear values to
its own ClutterLuminance.max, and add_luminance_mapping_op() maps a
luminance L of the source to L * target ref / source ref in the target:
conversions anchor reference white, so luminances of different states
are only comparable as multiples of their reference white. The three
states involved are:

 - the surface state, in the client's units: absolute for PQ (10000
   at the top, reference white 203 by default); its declared mastering
   luminance and MaxCLL are in the same units;
 - the output state, PQ with min 0.005, max 10000.005 and reference
   white 203 times the device's reference luminance factor; the
   firmware-declared target is in its units, display cd/m²;
 - the blending state derived from it: extended range gamma 2.2 sRGB,
   max 80 and reference white 80, so 80 of its units are the output's
   reference white (the forced-linear variant keeps the output's
   luminances).

So the blending state keeps the target in the display's units,
unscaled, and records the display's reference white luminance (the
output's reference luminance) as its display reference;
clutter_color_state_get_blending() now carries a source's target volume
and display reference into the blending state it derives. A state is a
display target only when it has both a target luminance and a display
reference above 0, which only such blending states have; a target
volume alone, as on a client state or an output state with the target
attached directly, is not mapped toward. The
predicate compares headrooms: the source's effective peak over its
reference white (target-volume values over its display reference,
which for a client state is its own reference white, the encoding
maximum over its reference white) against the target peak over the
display reference. That is the source peak as it lands on the display
after reference-white anchoring, compared with the target in cd/m²; it
holds for any reference luminance factor (SDR white at 203 times the
factor exceeds a target below that, and is then mapped). Computing both
sides with the same expression makes the comparison exact where it
matters: content declaring the published target as its own peak is not
mapped, and a blending state never exceeds its own target. Scaling the
target into blending units instead would have put a rounding error
(600 * 80 / 203) into exactly those comparisons.

For the curve, which is defined on absolute luminance, a blending
sample v is v * max / ref display references in cd/m², the source range
is its headroom range times the display reference, and the target peak
is used as declared. The transform from the blending state to
the output state never maps, because the output state declares no
target, so content is mapped once.

